WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:05.680
The Motion Picture Association of America
wields tremendous power in the film industry.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.690
Most major theater chains will refuse to screen
movies that haven't been rated by the MPAA,

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:15.629
or which have been assigned the dreaded NC-17
— meaning no minors, no matter what.

00:00:15.629 --> 00:00:20.410
Naturally, this system incentivizes both studios
and filmmakers to satisfy the ratings board's

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.410
standards.

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:26.210
But the anonymity and sometimes inscrutable
logic of the MPAA has led to a number of controversies.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:30.570
Here are the films that were slapped with
ratings that were too strict, too lenient,

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.360
or just plain strange.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.450
Hunchback's hypocrisy

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:40.040
Disney's animated output between the late
'80s and early '90s is referred to as a "renaissance"

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.040
for good reason.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.940
Beginning with The Little Mermaid and culminating
with The Lion King, their films racked up

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.960
critical praise, box office returns, and recognition
unheard of in decades.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:55.720
Then, in 2001, Atlantis: The Lost Empire ushered
in the era of PG being the norm for Disney.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.390
But just before the turn of the millennium,
The Hunchback of Notre Dame snuck through

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:00.690
with a pretty inappropriate G.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.830
"But I'd never fit in out there.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:05.600
I'm not normal…"

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.240
Although the studio streamlined the dense
original story from the 1831 novel, the villain

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.120
still confessed his lustful passions, in prayer,
during the "Hellfire" musical number.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.240
Some parents expressed concern, but not enough
to hold Hunchback back from financial success.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.420
A harrowing Hobbit

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:27.360
As the final film in Peter Jackson's two Middle-earth
trilogies, The Hobbit: The Battle of the Five

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.740
Armies holds a singular place in film history.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:33.790
Each Lord of the Rings and Hobbit film has
received an "Extended Edition" that includes

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:36.930
more material from Tolkien's books than its
theatrical counterpart.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:44.200
"You think your life is worth more than theirs,
when there is no love in it.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.920
There is no love in you."

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:52.280
The first five of these maintained their original
theatrical rating of PG-13, but Battle of

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:57.240
the Five Armies' bonus 20 minutes of footage,
got an R. An oddly severe rating, given that

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:01.210
Hobbit fans had likely already seen Return
of the King's depictions of severed heads

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:03.720
being flung by catapult.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.549
The too-dark Knight

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:09.810
One case of outrage over a movie's rating
was so severe it altered the course of an

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:11.780
entire multimedia franchise.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:17.090
Tim Burton's Batman became an instant pop
culture landmark in 1989 by presenting a more

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:19.120
serious take on the Caped Crusader.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.950
But many thought his second outing went too
far.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.670
"I am Cat Woman.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:26.720
Hear me roar!"

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.620
Batman Returns bears the same PG-13 rating
as its predecessor, but its script introduced

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.280
a heavy innuendo and psychosexual themes — and
then there was the Penguin, who had a tendency

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.970
to ooze black bile.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:41.380
According to Den of Geek, the backlash made
serious waves at Warner Bros.

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:46.069
And the reins to the next Batman adventure
would be handed over, instead, to Joel Schumacher,

00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:49.960
who delivered the bright neon action of Batman
Forever.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:51.540
Babe's barnyard carnage

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.750
George Miller may be known for his Mad Max
movies, but he's also made significant diversions

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:58.750
into family fare with the Happy Feet and Babe
series.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:03.459
The latter was a whimsical fable with a heart-warming
ending, but its G rating masks some surprisingly

00:03:03.459 --> 00:03:04.700
dark content.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.819
"I want my mom!"

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:11.349
True to Miller's style, Babe's themes run
deeper than meets the eye.

00:03:11.349 --> 00:03:15.319
Mortality looms over the barnyard, with most
of the characters reminding Babe that his

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:18.130
"purpose" is to be devoured by his human "bosses."

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:23.230
The Academy Awards even took note of Babe's
mature themes, nominating it for seven honors,

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.180
including Best Picture.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.420
Psycho sexuality

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:30.360
It was nearly impossible for American Psycho
to not cause controversy.

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:35.400
"One part of me wants to take her out and
talk to her, be real nice and sweet and treat

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.400
her right."

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.400
"And what does the other part think?"

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.290
Haha, what her head would look like on a stick!"

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:43.690
According to Variety, the film initially received
a rating of NC-17.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:48.540
Weirdly enough, though, what pushed it past
the R wasn't the graphic and gleeful murdering

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:52.930
committed by Christian Bale's character, but
a single non-violent sex scene.

00:03:52.930 --> 00:03:56.110
Director Mary Harron explained why she found
the rating objectionable, saying,

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:00.090
"That Bateman is looking at himself in the
mirror and not at his partners seems to be

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:04.820
an issue for the MPAA, but his expression
sums up his frighteningly detached relationship

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.050
to the world around him."

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:08.879
Rating the reality of Bully

00:04:08.879 --> 00:04:13.290
Documentaries present a unique challenge to
the MPAA, but no rating caused more of an

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.870
uproar than 2011's documentary Bully.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:21.980
"I feel kinda nervous going to school, because
I like learning, but I have trouble making

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:22.980
friends."

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.880
Director Lee Hirsch made the film to feature
issues faced by American children.

00:04:26.889 --> 00:04:32.120
But the MPAA declared Bully an R for language,
meaning the demographic depicted in the documentary

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.729
would be unable to see it without a parent,
and schools would be unlikely to host screenings.

00:04:36.729 --> 00:04:41.139
According to CinemaBlend, Michigan high school
student Katy Butler launched a Change.org

00:04:41.139 --> 00:04:45.199
petition that amassed over half a million
signatures protesting the rating.

00:04:45.199 --> 00:04:47.840
Still, the MPAA refused to budge.

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.849
Hirsch eventually prepared a PG-13 version
that toned down the language.

00:04:51.849 --> 00:04:57.349
The original cut was also released, but limited
to theaters willing to screen unrated films.

00:04:57.349 --> 00:04:58.860
A wilder bunch

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:05.270
In 1995, Warner Bros. prepared a "Director's
Cut" of Sam Peckinpah's 1969 classic The Wild

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:10.050
Bunch that restored ten minutes of material
trimmed from the original release for time.

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:13.780
"Relax, it's just some champagne we ordered."

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:19.330
An SFGate review praised the new release,
saying,

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:21.990
"The restored material does not call attention
to itself.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:24.460
It was there in the first place and it fits
right in."

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:30.219
But while The Wild Bunch was rated R in 1969,
the MPAA slapped the Director's Cut with an

00:05:30.219 --> 00:05:31.379
NC-17.

00:05:31.379 --> 00:05:35.710
Respected filmmakers fought the revisionist
rating, citing the fact that the NC-17 would

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:36.710
limit the film's availability.

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:41.400
The MPAA eventually relented to an appeal
from Warner Bros. and released the Director's

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:46.990
Cut with an R, but the incident still stands
as an example of the ratings board's imperfection.

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:47.990
Thanks for watching!

